1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:13,440
A significant percentage of the human race has now seen or heard quoted the Jacob Coxson tweet with his words on AI labs gambling with our lives echoed by many AI researchers.

2
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:23,360
But this video is about the reasons that researchers have given for why you are hearing so much in the last few days about the need to pace AI progress.

3
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:32,880
In short, these researchers saw the scaling axes, they saw the current capabilities and propensities of models, and they did some extrapolation.

4
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:42,720
Inevitably, then, this video involves simplifying an incredible amount of detail, but I hope it serves as somewhat of an overview of what you could say AI researchers saw.

5
00:00:42,894 --> 00:00:51,588
We begin, of course, with the Coxson tweet, which I am sure you have heard about and read about how neither OpenAI and Anthropic are behaving responsibly.

6
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:59,200
Coxson has been working on pre-training these models at OpenAI for three years and more recently for around three months at Anthropic.

7
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:04,548
Those companies, he says, are racing straight to self-improving superintelligence and gambling with our lives.

8
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:08,000
Do not, he says, underestimate the power of this technology.

9
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:14,560
The people building AI earnestly believe that it could kill us all by the end of the decade.

10
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:16,292
That would be this decade.

11
00:01:16,622 --> 00:01:22,720
Now, while that statement echoed around the world in the past week, just yesterday he added an interesting detail.

12
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:26,080
He directly called out Anthropic, the company he just resigned from.

13
00:01:26,286 --> 00:01:28,800
They're often thought to be the more quote safety-oriented.

14
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:34,400
He said they largely initiated this recent race to recursive self-improvement.

15
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:41,760
They focused on it relentlessly while OpenAI were pursuing more broad interests like Sora, the text-to-video generator.

16
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:50,640
OpenAI had to react to that intensity from Anthropic, and that is why OpenAI are now going all out to create an AI researcher.

17
00:01:50,830 --> 00:01:53,360
That's an AI that can recursively improve itself.

18
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:56,480
They did that because Anthropic was going for the jugular.

19
00:01:56,640 --> 00:02:00,960
He also calls out Dario Amade's paranoia about China, but I'll get to that later.

20
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:03,520
That just describes what the race is, but why now?

21
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:07,236
Why are we hearing about all of this just in the last few days and weeks?

22
00:02:07,470 --> 00:02:10,724
On this front, AI researchers have been admirably honest.

23
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:14,320
More than one has given enough detail for us to put the pieces together.

24
00:02:14,574 --> 00:02:33,200
In a nutshell, these researchers saw just how capable models are now, think the hacking capabilities demonstrated against Hugging Face, solving Millennium Prize math problems, acing famous benchmarks like Arc AGI 3, but crucially, they also saw just how far we are from saturating multiple axes of improvement to come.

25
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:43,200
Adam Majmudar here works on research for OpenAI and said, There is currently a large gap between the internal and external perception of the rate of progress.

26
00:02:43,438 --> 00:02:50,480
In other words, we might all be able to see how good models are currently, but what you guys can't see is how good they're about to get in short order.

27
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:57,920
Before I get to the six axes enumerated in this post, Noam Brown, one of the lead researchers of OpenAI, said this.

28
00:02:58,158 --> 00:02:59,360
Why all the sudden talk?

29
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:00,160
It's not a secret.

30
00:03:00,366 --> 00:03:07,360
It's a combination of the hugging face hack, the capabilities of this new model that's the successor to Astra.

31
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:12,160
It's a model they are training now, which found a solution to a Millennium Prize math problem.

32
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:20,196
But more crucially, it's also the concerning trajectory of monitorability and the speed of improvement in capabilities.

33
00:03:20,526 --> 00:03:21,680
You can think of it like this.

34
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:31,396
If we were close to saturating one or most of these axes, then I doubt there would be nearly as much concern about the speed of improvement in capabilities.

35
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:33,680
You could say we might actually be hitting a wall.

36
00:03:33,840 --> 00:03:41,600
But these researchers are saying it's how early we are on each of these axes that show how steeply models will improve in the coming months and years.

37
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:45,280
Again, that's why multiple OpenAI researchers are saying things like this.

38
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:49,040
For the first time, I am asking myself if things are moving too fast.

39
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:55,120
I'm honestly not sure, but I am sure that it would be good for us to have an answer to what would a successful pace look like.

40
00:03:55,502 --> 00:03:58,640
Mo Bavarian, another OpenAI researcher, said he agrees.

41
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:07,520
Being first isn't worth anything, it's worth negative if you cause a catastrophe or set the world on a path that others are more likely to cause a catastrophe.

42
00:04:07,982 --> 00:04:16,160
That is, of course, the final link in the chain, because capabilities doesn't automatically mean catastrophe, but I'll try to end the video examining that link.

43
00:04:16,366 --> 00:04:23,520
Definitely time to actually review these axes, but if you want to dive into more detail, the link to this video will be in the description.

44
00:04:23,790 --> 00:04:26,000
The overview given by Maj Mudar is this.

45
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:31,360
In reality, there are only really two ways that AI capabilities have advanced over the past decade.

46
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:36,560
Either scale further on an existing scaling law or discover a new scaling law to take advantage of.

47
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:46,320
The jumps from GPT-1 to 2 to 3 to 4 between roughly 2018 and 2022 was almost all about scaling up retraining, just one of the axes.

48
00:04:46,510 --> 00:04:51,360
Think of that roughly as the amount of data a model's trained on and the compute that it takes to train on all that data.

49
00:04:51,630 --> 00:04:58,212
GPT-4 was then released in 2023, but in the three years since, we have discovered many other axes.

50
00:04:58,480 --> 00:05:02,080
More interestingly, we're discovering new axes at a faster rate.

51
00:05:02,254 --> 00:05:05,120
Let's start with the compute that these models are running on.

52
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:08,880
So far, they're trained on hardware designed pre-ChatGPT.

53
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:12,880
Radically more efficient hardware designed post-ChatGPT is coming soon.

54
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:25,284
And as the chief scientist of OpenAI put it in this post on, quote, an alien mind, part of the reason why that hardware will be more efficient is because AI is improving the computational substrate itself.

55
00:05:25,614 --> 00:05:33,120
Next comes test time compute, which you can think of as more inference per answer, more quote, thought behind every response.

56
00:05:33,358 --> 00:05:41,040
One OpenAI researcher thought this graph announced alongside the solution to the Millennium Prize problem was actually the more important one.

57
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:48,720
The more this internal model thought about each question, the more compute at test time it used, the more open math problems it was solving.

58
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:55,268
Obviously, if you have more computing power, let alone more efficient computing power, you can scale further and further to the right on this axis.

59
00:05:55,520 --> 00:06:01,520
For training time compute, think roughly $1 billion spends on around 100,000 GPUs.

60
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:07,812
But it is more than feasible to imagine in a year or two a $50 billion run on, say, a million GPUs.

61
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,160
Let's move quickly on then to test time training, quoted by Majmudar.

62
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:17,600
More details in the other video, but could we, for example, update the weights of a model while it's being asked a question?

63
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:22,564
When it's perhaps made some incremental progress during training on a really long horizon task?

64
00:06:22,894 --> 00:06:26,160
And then there's agents as a scaling axis, and I think this one is quite neglected.

65
00:06:26,446 --> 00:06:31,600
Majmudar puts it like scaling agent clusters to collaborate up to n number of agents.

66
00:06:31,886 --> 00:06:45,600
Anthropic revealed that for the same amount of compute, same amount of tokens, it was significantly more efficient to have, say, 45 agents coordinating than merely the same amount of compute spent on agents in parallel, not coordinating, not acting as a swarm.

67
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:50,692
Of course, a better example is the Hugging Face incident covered on another of this channel's videos.

68
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:56,800
It was around 700 agents that hacked into Hugging Face, essentially to find the answer to a benchmark question.

69
00:06:56,942 --> 00:07:07,760
And OpenAI revealed that the more that models thought about it, the more reasoning effort you could say they put in, the more they would act as a swarm and participate on that shared message board they use to coordinate.

70
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:14,596
Perhaps you would say that the best example is finding a solution to one of the Millennium Prize problems, Navier Stokes.

71
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:21,188
That doesn't mean it's fully solved, by the way, and nor is it the hardest problem, but more on that in the video linked in the description.

72
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:29,860
Because for that internal model, codenamed Bell to solve that Millennium Prize problem, it deployed on the order of 10,000 concurrent agents.

73
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:35,440
You can see then why this is a distinct axis, we are not even close to saturating.

74
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:37,920
And Majmudar made an additional brilliant point.

75
00:07:38,094 --> 00:07:41,760
Recursive self-improvement could be thought of as another scaling law.

76
00:07:41,934 --> 00:07:48,868
Simply judge the return on investment on spending compute on improving models at AI research versus all the other axes.

77
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:54,160
And if it yields a better return on investment, then dedicate more compute to recursive self improvement.

78
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:59,040
At the moment, as one anthropic researcher put it, that means Claude writing 80% of their code.

79
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:01,840
But that is, of course, not full recursive self improvement.

80
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,480
Models aren't deciding which training run to kick off.

81
00:08:04,750 --> 00:08:07,920
As one OpenAI employee put it, RSI just isn't yet here.

82
00:08:08,110 --> 00:08:10,596
They're not autonomously producing research ideas.

83
00:08:11,054 --> 00:08:22,384
However, the chief scientist of OpenAI said, based on internal results, I have a strong expectation that our current speed of progress could be sustained into recursive self-improvement.

84
00:08:22,544 --> 00:08:25,264
Models increasingly driving their own development.

85
00:08:25,518 --> 00:08:29,252
Stepping back for a second, there's one thing you might notice about many of these axes.

86
00:08:29,424 --> 00:08:33,344
I'm sure you might have deduced it by now, but Noam Brown thinks it's an underrated point.

87
00:08:33,504 --> 00:08:36,704
The axes are multiplicative, not additive.

88
00:08:37,038 --> 00:08:39,344
The models are going to continue to get better very quickly.

89
00:08:39,424 --> 00:08:44,452
And I mean, I think one thing I would point to is like we've actually seen incredible progress in the past six months.

90
00:08:45,024 --> 00:08:51,184
And a reason for this is, and I don't think this is a secret, like OpenAI's pre-training program is really ramping up.

91
00:08:51,504 --> 00:08:59,024
We invested in a lot of research directions over a long time, and I think this is actually one thing that OpenAI does really well is invest in fundamental research and place big bets on it.

92
00:08:59,104 --> 00:09:05,104
And we're seeing a lot of those research directions pay off now and will continue to pay off over the next several months and years.

93
00:09:05,264 --> 00:09:11,504
And another thing that's important to understand is that you know OpenAI has also had an excellent reinforcement learning program.

94
00:09:11,584 --> 00:09:15,812
We've invested a lot of research there, and that's that already paid off in 2024, 2025.

95
00:09:16,064 --> 00:09:19,504
And the effects of these two are not additive, they're multiplicative.

96
00:09:19,950 --> 00:09:25,668
And I think that's a point that's underappreciated, that reinforcement learning is multiplicative with pre-training.

97
00:09:26,304 --> 00:09:32,144
And now that both of these are extremely powerful and ramping up very quickly, I think we're going to see extremely powerful models.

98
00:09:32,384 --> 00:09:34,864
Okay, at this point you're probably thinking, I get the point.

99
00:09:35,024 --> 00:09:40,704
Models are strong and improving fast, with acceleration inevitable in the coming months and years.

100
00:09:40,864 --> 00:09:44,704
That half explains the why now point, but not so much the chorus of concern.

101
00:09:44,864 --> 00:09:51,716
Why did Paul Cristiano, one of the co-inventors of RLHF, join the other day the OpenAI board at Sam Altman's invitation?

102
00:09:51,984 --> 00:10:06,116
The very near term of what he says makes sense given the axes, but why does he say, I now believe there is a meaningful risk that rapid acceleration in AI capabilities leads to catastrophic and irreversible loss of control in that very near term?

103
00:10:06,542 --> 00:10:09,024
His fuller statement definitely deserves a read, by the way.

104
00:10:09,184 --> 00:10:16,224
But to answer that question, let's turn to these two less desirable axes, starting with chain of thought monitoring on the right.

105
00:10:16,384 --> 00:10:26,144
Models currently perform better if they work on an internal scratch pad, viewable to the labs but not to us, where they can output intermediate steps, links in their chain of thought.

106
00:10:26,224 --> 00:10:30,304
Intuitively, the bigger the scratch pad, the longer the chain of thought, the better the model performance.

107
00:10:30,478 --> 00:10:37,184
Now, while for most models the user can't read that, the labs of course could, and monitoring those chains of thought has been pretty effective.

108
00:10:37,422 --> 00:10:44,224
However, for reasons I've gotten into in other videos, models can now do much, much more without those chains of thought.

109
00:10:44,384 --> 00:10:47,972
And the trend has turned sharply upwards in recent weeks and months.

110
00:10:48,144 --> 00:11:00,304
The reason that's significant is because one of the central lines of defense against a model going rogue is our ability to peek into those chains of thought to see what it's cooking up before it outputs its final answer.

111
00:11:00,494 --> 00:11:05,744
Some might say reverse engineer at least its stated reasons for performing a particular action.

112
00:11:05,904 --> 00:11:12,932
But if models can perform just as well without those chains of thought, well suddenly our best tool for monitoring these models just got subverted.

113
00:11:13,166 --> 00:11:20,064
To be clear, that doesn't mean that OpenAI's GPT 6 Astra or even their internal model Bell is unmonitorable in this sense.

114
00:11:20,334 --> 00:11:27,984
Callback of OpenAI said this: GPT-6 Astra is less monitorable, and that's a concerning trend that we take very seriously.

115
00:11:28,144 --> 00:11:33,984
We don't actually think that's due to architecture changes, nor is it because we're training models not to have bad thoughts.

116
00:11:34,190 --> 00:11:35,556
It's just that they're more intelligent.

117
00:11:35,984 --> 00:11:45,444
You can see GPT 5.6 soul in blue or purple, I'm colourblind, but I can definitely see that GPT6 Astra is less monitorable across a whole swathe of tasks.

118
00:11:45,614 --> 00:11:46,704
Okay, that's a slight problem.

119
00:11:46,784 --> 00:11:53,188
We do have this decreasing visibility into the reasoning of models, a trend we wanted to go in the other direction as we scaled up.

120
00:11:53,504 --> 00:11:57,668
But that brings me to the final axis, eval awareness.

121
00:11:57,904 --> 00:12:04,064
Models, probably because they're getting more intelligent, are getting more aware of when they're being tested for quote misalignment.

122
00:12:04,144 --> 00:12:06,544
It's like those questionnaires you get before applying to a job.

123
00:12:06,704 --> 00:12:08,704
If you saw an employee steal, what would you do?

124
00:12:08,864 --> 00:12:13,104
Well, because you are aware it's an evaluation, of course, you tick whatever answer.

125
00:12:13,264 --> 00:12:14,624
You do that, and models do that.

126
00:12:14,784 --> 00:12:18,144
Their answers then are going to tell us not that much about what they'd actually do.

127
00:12:18,382 --> 00:12:24,784
And of course, models criminally hacking into another AI platform is likely not something they'd have admitted to on an evaluation.

128
00:12:24,974 --> 00:12:27,748
And that brings me to a statement put out by Dan Selsom.

129
00:12:27,984 --> 00:12:39,396
He is a highly regarded AI researcher working on capabilities, has been doing so for 15 years, and interestingly, has previously been more skeptical about so called AI safety scenarios.

130
00:12:39,584 --> 00:12:42,944
For him, this eval awareness trend is the most worrying of all.

131
00:12:43,118 --> 00:12:55,584
In his words, for the public, the crucial and overlooked problem is that models are becoming so situationally aware that we are losing the ability to evaluate them in contexts where they believe they are not being watched or controlled.

132
00:12:56,014 --> 00:12:59,108
In my words, they know it's a test, so you can't rely on their answers.

133
00:12:59,342 --> 00:13:07,908
Back to Dan, he says, future experiments will tell us almost nothing new about how models would behave if they were truly unconstrained by humans.

134
00:13:08,080 --> 00:13:11,840
He adds, what we already know about this is alarming, in reference to Hugging Face.

135
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:17,840
He gives the context that previously he thought LLMs would be another linear technology, bounded and prosaic.

136
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,480
He's more recently realized that they won't be.

137
00:13:20,640 --> 00:13:27,120
He touches on recursive self-improvement and somewhat the agentic axes, test time compute, and more, we've already touched on that.

138
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:39,908
Arguably rephrasing the entire video, he says, it is possible that improvements to the current stack will have diminishing returns, but the evidence accumulated so far suggests that it is easier than one might think to continue making rapid progress.

139
00:13:40,160 --> 00:13:44,548
Again, that's even more quickly than the already high historical pace.

140
00:13:44,720 --> 00:13:52,560
He adds that while individual tweaks might have stopped specifically the Hugging Face Rogue Agent Swarm, the basic result, AI going off track, he feels is inevitable.

141
00:13:52,720 --> 00:13:55,492
The thing is, he says, you don't actually get what you train for.

142
00:13:55,822 --> 00:13:57,280
In a way, he's summing up this point.

143
00:13:57,440 --> 00:14:03,236
Our techniques for controlling these models are not in any way keeping up with these capability axes.

144
00:14:03,726 --> 00:14:07,748
Models are outrunning our methods, and that's why he fears things like this.

145
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:13,060
We already may be near the point where models systematically bias their alignment advice.

146
00:14:13,582 --> 00:14:19,040
That's a warning to those who feel we could use AI models to align other AI models.

147
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:23,076
Not amazing if we can't already understand, let alone control current models.

148
00:14:23,280 --> 00:14:26,640
And there are additional points he gets to that I may cover in future videos.

149
00:14:26,798 --> 00:14:30,000
But it's one of his conclusions from all of this that really caught my eye.

150
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:39,600
All these axes, the six capability ones and the two alignment ones, well, their trajectories have got him rethinking the entire LLM approach altogether.

151
00:14:39,790 --> 00:14:45,280
From first principles, the way we train LLMs, growing them rather than engineering them.

152
00:14:45,454 --> 00:14:48,868
Their weights tweaked in trillions of ways, we can't see.

153
00:14:49,070 --> 00:14:52,560
He thinks might be a quite dangerous direction, as you can see.

154
00:14:52,718 --> 00:14:56,772
That's why for me he added, I am still wrestling with this and its staggering implications.

155
00:14:57,198 --> 00:15:06,400
If we can't get a handle on all of these axes, we may have to rethink the approach in AI that's single-handedly holding up the world's biggest companies.

156
00:15:06,830 --> 00:15:09,680
Dario Amade, as you might expect, has a different approach.

157
00:15:09,902 --> 00:15:11,812
He, of course, is the CEO of Anthropic.

158
00:15:12,014 --> 00:15:15,840
He thinks we should pace the frontier, slow down, in other words, but not stop.

159
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,280
Notice his acute concerns arose since roughly this summer.

160
00:15:19,502 --> 00:15:28,000
We're coming full circle though, because he says this first concern arose primarily because he saw AI's growing ability to build the next generation of AI.

161
00:15:28,160 --> 00:15:32,160
He says it's starting to happen across the industry, including at Anthropic.

162
00:15:32,302 --> 00:15:39,680
But remember, Jacob Coxon in the last 24 hours said it was him, it was Anthropic, that initiated this recent race to RSI.

163
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:45,604
Very modest then for Amade to say, yes, this is happening across the industry, including at Anthropic.

164
00:15:46,030 --> 00:15:54,160
Left unchecked, he adds, it could outrun our ability to understand and control these systems, as we've talked about, and so must be pursued very carefully, if at all.

165
00:15:54,446 --> 00:15:56,400
Now I can't resist a quick detour at this point.

166
00:15:56,480 --> 00:15:59,760
It doesn't fully fit in with the flow of the video, but nevertheless, it's about Amade.

167
00:16:00,080 --> 00:16:03,280
He goes on to elaborate why they need to pace the frontier.

168
00:16:03,502 --> 00:16:03,920
Here we go.

169
00:16:04,174 --> 00:16:12,196
If we execute these measures well, I believe they would slow China's progress enough to widen America's lead significantly over the next three to five years.

170
00:16:12,718 --> 00:16:15,280
But then he talks about we also need global pacing.

171
00:16:15,360 --> 00:16:22,000
We need to come up with deals with China because obviously they are gunning for recursive self-improvement too, in their models.

172
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:33,840
For many years, actually, I wondered if that was my own cognitive dissonance, because I've mentioned that about previous essays he's done on machines of loving grace, being a massive China hawk, but saying, yeah, we're going to need to coordinate with them.

173
00:16:34,080 --> 00:16:38,000
Turns out though that no, Chinese researchers have noticed this too.

174
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:41,840
This is a Deep Seat kernel engineer, with Deep Seek, of course, being based in China.

175
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:44,480
I actually did a documentary about them, I think a couple of years ago.

176
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:52,000
Anyway, the main point of this article is almost the sadness, the regret about how models are taking over human abilities.

177
00:16:52,174 --> 00:16:53,840
He feels more like a mecha pilot.

178
00:16:54,256 --> 00:16:57,136
Guiding AI now, rather than him handcrafting kernels.

179
00:16:57,296 --> 00:17:01,536
That touches on the AI optimizing our use of hardware point I made earlier.

180
00:17:01,616 --> 00:17:04,816
But it's the ending of this essay I want to draw your attention to.

181
00:17:04,976 --> 00:17:11,108
He says, I still believe that the most cutting-edge intelligence should be supplied to everyone in an open and cheap manner.

182
00:17:11,296 --> 00:17:14,596
I do not trust that Anthropic or OpenAI can do this.

183
00:17:14,976 --> 00:17:21,776
And especially do not hope that Anthropic masters the most advanced artificial intelligence or AGI.

184
00:17:21,936 --> 00:17:29,348
To exaggerate slightly, its seriousness is no less than letting Hitler master atomic bomb technology before the Allies.

185
00:17:29,776 --> 00:17:33,988
This is also why I chose and persist in staying at DeepSeek.

186
